explanation:
Following major injury, protracted surgery in a physiologically unstable patient can in itself prove fatal. Patients with the 'deadly triad' of hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy are those at highest risk. 'Damage control' or 'damage limitation surgery' is the minimum surgery needed to stabilise the patient's condition and the safest course until the physiological derangement can be corrected. Damage control surgery is restricted to two goals:
1. Stopping any active surgical bleeding;
2. Controlling any contamination.
